ered this  cost of exploration  so essentially what I'm saying is that I  think about online learning  um essentially as a kind of like a cost  allocation problem right so exploration  is something that's necessary It's  associated with the cost and some people  have to incur this cost and uh different  banded algorithms or different online  learning policies might allocate those  costs to different populations  so in our work with my advisor Rebecca  fryas what we do is we study how to  allocate this cost of exploration in a  quote-unquote fair manner so I'm going  to kind of briefly describe the main  results of this work  so the first result is that we show that  canonical algorithms like UCB are unfair  and uh to explain what I mean by this  let me give you a very uh let me walk  through a very simple concrete example  so here in this example we have we have  the canonical multi-arm Bandit problem  like the K arm band a problem with three  arms but I'm adding the notion of groups  and here the notion of groups there's  groups A and B at each time step one of  the two groups arrive and this bipartite  graph specifies which uh arms a  particular group has access to so if  group a arrives uh the central decision  maker has to assign them uh has to take  one of the actions that are available  for this group so this is a very simple  example with just three arms and two  groups and in this example only arm two  is a shared arm between the two groups  and so if we want to learn about arm2  you know we can either get group a to  pull it or we can get Group D to pull it  um and so on  um but if we assume let's let's assume  that  um the rewards satisfy this uh this  relationship so arm two is actually the  worst arm followed by arm one followed  by arm3 in this case you see that  there's actually an asymmetry in how  much regret is incurred when uh both of  these groups pull on two right because  essentially the kind of the other option  for group a is worse than the ot